am has been approaching  me about Mosul and about how can we have  it so like close to home but look like  come from Mosul south it could look like  I'm from Iraq south and when I read the  script and when I when I look how Mathew  want to project in how he's honest and  he want to give right to those voice of  the policeman that tried to liberate  Mosul  Isis from - I was really tried to put  any offer I have any experience I have  to put it behind the project and to be  to be to be like so close - you feel it  like close to home and the most  important things we have it in great  great like a screening in Venice and  when the journalist came back to us and  asked us I asked a question what did he  said he saw a rocky film or an American  film and people came to me and said to  me like it's a rocky films look like  shorten it I could look like the actor  Arrakis and this is really we give a  credit to the to the to the John Anthony  and tomato and to all team behind this  project what would you say is the  biggest misconception about Mosul in  that area that you want people to know  about I think one of the for us there's  there's many things but I think is the  biggest misconception is just it's  missing the very human stories that are  happening there that's the big and that  that's why I responded so strongly to  the to the original article for years  now I've been obsessed with the news you  know Joe and I are news junkies we read  everything and you know that's been a  large part of our our work for the past  several years is figuring out how we  sort of bring these collective  experiences that we're all having  globally into our storytelling and this  seemed like an area of the world we're  really important and extreme things were  happening yet it was so hard to  understand that on human level and I  think that's partially the nature of  warfare you know the nature of warfare  is it's it's a hard place to access  because it's dangerous and restricted  and al