 of the iceberg in  terms of what types of stories are  waiting there for us to explore if we  simply open our open our open our eyes  to them and I think so the virtue of  this movie is it's going to give you an  experience in the cinema that you  literally never had I feel like there's  no one answer to this but it's on my  mind because you guys are here bringing  this movie to the public after having  had the biggest box-office hit of all  time so why is it important to you to  make sure there's that kind of balance  not just in your careers but in the  industry a movie like Avengers endgame  that really means a lot to a whole lot  of people and then these really  important true stories that you need to  bring awareness to well media is a very  powerful tool and it can be used either  negatively reinforced or positively  reinforced and and we want to use it  effectively to positively reinforce and  also just just from you know a pure  standpoint of as storytellers and  filmmakers getting to understand what's  going on there in a very intimate level  collaborating I mean I think the way  that we will we will ultimately come to  heal and understand each other on a  global scales by telling each other  stories and collaborating to tell each  other stories and that was the most  important mission of this project for us  I think just to add something muscle is  making history for American cinema  because we can say I mean I can say as a  Arabic person this is the first time in  American history we have a film to make  the Arabic character is a hero you know  it's like a hero like a normal human  being like normal any policeman in the  world this is the first film to show the  Muslim and positive side you know and  and there's the first film to be to be  to be the main character speaking their  language Arabic language and I think  this is very important step in American  history of cinema because if I ask you  our question how many film you sell  about Arabic character positive you  you're shaking your head yeah so Musa is  really a history in the top of that we  have a great story and a great black  production and and I'm telling you from  from from a filmmaker that was part of  the team to make this film I saw the  film twice in each time I'm so connected  to the store it so connected by the end  I start to cry no just like feel wow it  looked like those policemen that we are  telling because based on the true story  those policemen that they were fighting  for to liberate the city yeah it looked  like they are fighting in the name of  international community because this is  what Alec was used to tell us we are  fighting in the name of the  international community so when you see  this film you are getting justice and  this justice is very close to you so you  guys all wholeheartedly believed in this  idea being a movie just from reading the  article after you gone through this  whole process you've got a final cut is  there anything about the finished  product that pleasantly surprised you  something that you grew to appreciate  that you never  but knew you did early on I don't know  if we're surprised by it but I think the  performances are exceptional I really do  I think and I think Suhail our lead his  performance is profound in the movie  it's as good as the performances I think  you'll see this year and and I think his  story his his true story the flight from  Iraq and ending up in in New Mexico in  the US and and having to give up acting  for the most part and and then you know  playing the lead in this movie is is is  incredibly touching for us and and as  Mohammed said when we watch the film all  of that comes to bear for us as  filmmakers and producers and I you know  I cry every time I watch the movie -  because I your feeling everything that's  happening with this film from its it's  as you say a historic approach to  storytelling to the the lives of the  actors in this movie coming into the  film and coming to 